\newlabel{fig:stanford_res}{{4}{5}{Ablation experiments for understanding the key factors contribute to the performance gain. The comparison of baseline and BT indicates that even error-prone pseudo-parallel data are beneficial due to the injected implicit knowledge of unseen combinations (Section \ref {section:static}). The comparison of BT and BT+OTF indicates that perturbations brought by the on-the-fly mechanism can prevent models learning specific incorrect bias, thus improving the performance (Section \ref {section:otf}). IBT performs best because it uses both source-side and target-side monolingual data while others use only target-side monolingual data.}{}{}}
